WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Almost every intrusion you'll ever investigate touches identity.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:11.180
An attacker with a stolen credential needs no exploit — they just log in, and to your logs they look like a normal user.

3
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.550
This is why identity is the single most important domain to understand deeply.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:23.490
If you can't read a logon event, the exact records that describe a domain compromise will look perfectly normal to you.

5
00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:30.010
Four words this whole topic rests on. Identity is the claim — "I am jsmith."

6
00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.260
Authentication is the proof — actually demonstrating that claim.

7
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.720
Authorization is what that identity is then allowed to do.

8
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.230
And accounting is the log trail of what they actually did — your evidence.

9
00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.700
On a Windows domain, Kerberos is how you log in. It's four steps.

10
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:53.440
The user proves who they are to the domain controller and gets a ticket-granting ticket — a TGT.

11
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.980
To reach a service, they present the TGT and request a service ticket.

12
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:07.290
That ticket unlocks the service. NTLM is the older challenge-response fallback — weaker, and a red flag where Kerberos should be used.

13
00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:17.290
So what do you watch for? An RC4-encrypted service ticket where stronger AES is expected — the signature of Kerberoasting.

14
00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:22.070
Many failed logons for many accounts from one source — a password spray.

15
00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:27.690
NTLM showing up where Kerberos should be — a downgrade that can signal relay abuse.

16
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:33.620
And impossible travel — the same account logging in from two places too far apart to be real.

17
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:40.960
Every logon event tells you how someone got in: the logon type, the authentication package, and the source.

18
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.100
Learn to read those three, and a domain compromise stops hiding in plain sight.
